>> title: Hand-kneaded pan pizza
>> author: Easy Recipes
>> image: photos/pan-pizza.webp
>> photo by: Easy Recipes
>> servings: 3
>> time: about 5 hours, mostly rising
>> cuisine: Italian
>> category: Vegetarian, Starter
>> tags: Weekend, Make ahead
>> description: Soft, airy pizza baked in a tray, kneaded by hand with dry yeast and regular Gallo flour.

In a large #bowl{}, dissolve @dry yeast{2%g} in @water at room temperature{330%ml} (up to 370 ml if the dough absorbs it). Add @extra virgin olive oil{15%ml} and @salt{10%g}, and stir until the salt has dissolved.

Add @Gallo flour{500%g} a little at a time and mix by hand for a few minutes, until you have a rough dough with no dry flour left.

Cover the bowl and let it rest for ~{10%minutes}.

With wet hands, pull one side of the dough up and fold it over, turn the bowl and repeat 4 times. Cover and rest for another ~{10%minutes}. Repeat folds + rest 2–3 times in total, until the dough is smooth.

Cover and let it rise at room temperature until doubled in size, usually 2–3 hours depending on how warm your home is: ~{2.5%hours}.

Divide the dough in two: a 550 g ball for the 40×30 tray and one with the rest. Tuck the edges underneath to make smooth balls.

Oil the trays with a drizzle of olive oil and place the dough balls in them. Cover and let them rise until doubled, about 1–1.5 hours: ~{75%minutes}. Then stretch the dough with your fingertips to the edges of the tray.

Heat the #oven{} to 250°C. Spread @tomato passata{200%g} over the dough with a pinch of salt and a drizzle of oil, and bake for about 15 minutes in total: first ~{10%minutes}.

For the last ~{5%minutes} of baking, add @mozzarella{250%g} in small pieces and finish baking.

> Flour: regular Gallo flour is weaker than the 00 W270 of the original recipe and absorbs less water, so start with 330 ml. If the dough is easy to work, add the rest of the water little by little, up to 370 ml.
> If you find Gallo "harina de fuerza" (strong flour), you can go straight to 370 ml of water.
> Keep a little extra olive oil for greasing the trays.
> Serves 3: the large tray can be shared by two, the small one is a pizza for one.
